  "slug": "learning-causation-2f3a",
  "summary": "New tests attach null models to spatial domains and scRNA-seq clusters, while audits expose where predictions and sequencing depth fall short.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is replacing heuristic partitions with explicitly tested ones. SigDoTS applies hypothesis testing with FWER control to spatial domain detection, using null simulations to merge or retain candidate domains. ProClust tests scRNA-seq cluster significance, merging overclustered groups through log-concave cluster modelling and projection-based testing. CellSavior brings confidence-weighted merging to ensemble clustering for cell calling in targeted scDNA-seq. Other items address validity and standardization: the AlphaGenome perturbation analysis finds CAGE and RNA-seq predictions decouple across 2,111 promoter-gene pairs, the splicing study shows typical RNA-seq depths miss alternative splicing, and SigBridgeR unifies eight phenotype-associated subpopulation algorithms for benchmarking. What is new is that partitioning outputs now come with an explicit null or error model, alongside systematic audits of model and depth limits.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-15"
